---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../lib/paths";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial standards, corrections & privacy"
  description="How to read Independent Observer status labels, request corrections, understand browser-local tools, and ask about reuse or disclosures."
  currentPath="/governance/"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Standards & trust"
    title="Editorial standards, corrections & privacy"
    description="A compact guide to editorial standards, corrections, privacy, reuse, and disclosures."
  />
  <section class="section section-white">
    <div class="shell prose">
      <p class="review-date"><strong>Last reviewed:</strong> 19 September 2026</p>
      <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
      <p>
        Independent Observer separates evidence, interpretation, and proposals. Public records
        identify sources and limitations where available. Working papers, concept previews, and
        books in editorial development retain their status labels; availability on this site does
        not imply peer review or a finished book.
      </p>
      <h2>Status labels</h2>
      <p>
        Status labels describe what a reader can use now; they do not grade the quality of an idea
        or substitute for independent review.
      </p>
      <dl class="status-definitions">
        <dt>Concept preview</dt>
        <dd>
          A short public outline introduces an idea that has not yet become a finished paper or
          publication.
        </dd>
        <dt>In editorial development</dt>
        <dd>
          The author is still developing, checking, structuring, or placing the work before release.
        </dd>
        <dt>Research preview</dt>
        <dd>
          A public research map presents a question, mechanism, or evidence boundary without
          claiming a completed study.
        </dd>
        <dt>Author working paper / working paper</dt>
        <dd>
          A dated author manuscript is available as work in progress and has not been externally
          peer reviewed.
        </dd>
        <dt>Public author paper / public paper record</dt>
        <dd>
          A public record points to an author paper and its available metadata without implying
          independent validation.
        </dd>
        <dt>Public reading copy</dt>
        <dd>
          A readable site edition presents public material while preserving the source, status, and
          stated limitations.
        </dd>
        <dt>Reviewed public copy</dt>
        <dd>
          A site reading copy has passed the recorded internal publication checks but has not
          thereby received external peer review.
        </dd>
        <dt>Public summary</dt>
        <dd>
          A short explanation describes a larger work without reproducing or releasing the complete
          source document.
        </dd>
        <dt>Public learning exercise</dt>
        <dd>
          An instructional example helps readers test a method and is not presented as new empirical
          evidence.
        </dd>
        <dt>Editorial framework</dt>
        <dd>
          A public guide explains how the site organizes or evaluates work rather than presenting a
          research finding.
        </dd>
        <dt>Mapped research direction / research-map entry</dt>
        <dd>
          A catalogue entry shows where a proposed line of inquiry belongs, but it is not itself a
          released paper.
        </dd>
        <dt>Preview-only bounded text adaptation</dt>
        <dd>
          A limited text adaptation is available for review or preview and is not included in the
          formal release feed.
        </dd>
        <dt>Published bounded text adaptation</dt>
        <dd>
          A limited, source-bounded text adaptation has passed the site’s recorded release checks
          and is publicly listed.
        </dd>
        <dt>Preview article / preview-only article</dt>
        <dd>
          These shorter interface labels refer to a preview-only bounded text adaptation that has
          not entered the formal release feed.
        </dd>
        <dt>Published article / published document</dt>
        <dd>
          These shorter interface labels identify material that has passed the site’s recorded
          release gate and is included in public discovery.
        </dd>
        <dt>Editorial preview</dt>
        <dd>
          An author-produced media or editorial item is available as a preview while further work
          may continue.
        </dd>
        <dt>Publication pending</dt>
        <dd>The referenced item is not yet released for public reading on this site.</dd>
      </dl>
      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        To question a claim or report an error, use the <a href={sitePath("/contact/")}
          >contact page</a
        >. Include the page address, the passage, and evidence supporting a correction. Public paper
        revisions are listed when recorded. An approved correction is marked in the affected page’s <strong
          >Revision history</strong
        > with its date, change, reason, and a link to the affected section. The same paper correction
        also appears in the <a href={sitePath("/latest/#latest-revisions-title")}>revision log</a>;
        software and interface changes appear in <a href={sitePath("/whats-new/")}>What’s New</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="todo-notice">
        <strong>TODO (owner):</strong> choose and publish a correction-inquiry response-time commitment.
      </p>
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Search runs in your browser after downloading a public index. Search queries are not
        submitted to a search server. Reading lists, tags, and reading status use local browser
        storage; you can clear saved items or export them. Imported reading lists are processed on
        your device.
      </p>
      <p>
        The contact page prepares an email draft or copies text for your webmail. It does not submit
        a form to this website. Your email provider handles messages you send. Visiting the website
        still requires ordinary network requests to its hosting provider. External links open
        services with their own privacy practices.
      </p>
      <h3>Reader list and hosting</h3>
      <p>
        The reader-list links currently lead to Substack. This site does not collect the email
        address itself; Substack handles any subscription under its own privacy practices. The site
        is hosted on Vercel, so ordinary page requests are processed through Vercel’s hosting
        infrastructure.
      </p>
      <h3>Analytics, cookies, and browser storage</h3>
      <p>
        The repository contains no production analytics provider or analytics script. Its production
        event adapter is a no-op, so the site does not send analytics events. The site code does not
        set cookies. It does use local storage for reader-controlled conveniences such as saved
        reading, listening progress, and “new since your last visit,” plus session storage for the
        current search state. External services reached through links may use their own analytics,
        cookies, or storage.
      </p>
      <h2>Copyright and reuse</h2>
      <p>
        Public access does not itself grant permission to republish material. Follow any rights or
        licence notice attached to an item. Source material and linked third-party work may have
        separate rights. For permission or a rights concern, identify the material and intended use
        through the <a href={sitePath("/contact/")}>contact page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="todo-notice">
        <strong>TODO (owner):</strong> choose the default licence for site-authored material; until then,
        no additional default reuse licence is granted beyond any item-specific notice.
      </p>
      <h2>Disclosures</h2>
      <h3>Authorship</h3>
      <p>
        Independent Observer is a single-author research and media project by Siddhartha Harsh
        Wardhan. The site’s papers, previews, and adaptations have no external peer review unless a
        specific page explicitly records otherwise.
      </p>
      <h3>Funding</h3>
      <p class="todo-notice"><strong>TODO (owner):</strong> disclose how the project is funded.</p>
      <h3>Revenue</h3>
      <p class="todo-notice">
        <strong>TODO (owner):</strong> disclose current and planned revenue sources.
      </p>
      <h3>Conflicts of interest</h3>
      <p class="todo-notice">
        <strong>TODO (owner):</strong> publish a conflicts-of-interest statement.
      </p>
      <h3>Use of AI tools</h3>
      <p class="todo-notice">
        <strong>TODO (owner):</strong> disclose which AI tools, if any, are used and how their output
        is reviewed before publication.
      </p>
      <p>
        Links to source institutions or distribution platforms do not imply endorsement. Historical
        downloads and views are dated distribution signals, not evidence of scholarly quality.
        Publication status is stated on each record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .review-date {
    padding-bottom: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .status-definitions dt {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-weight: 800;
  }
  .status-definitions dd {
    margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
  }
  .todo-notice {
    padding: 0.9rem 1rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--gold);
    background: var(--paper-muted, #f4f1e8);
  }
</style>
